[Historical Record] Per the Topic10 query instruction, below are the disaster records for three key periods of this country (on a scale of a thousand people or more):

1. Ancient Dynasty Period (1500–900 BC): In 1342 BC the Yellow River changed course and flooded; the cause was destruction of upstream vegetation plus concentrated torrential rain, the consequence was that 3 downstream city-states were inundated, with about 21,000 people dead and agricultural losses accounting for 15% of the national total, and the lasting after-effect was regional soil salinization persisting for 50 years;
2. Medieval City-State Alliance Period (AD 500-1200): In AD 876 the Black Death epidemic occurred; the cause was plague bacilli introduced along trade routes, the consequence was a national population decline of 18% (about 120,000 deaths), with commerce stagnating for 6 years;
3. Modern Industrialization Period (AD 1800-1950): In AD 1923 a coal mine gas explosion accident occurred; the cause was absent safety procedures plus over-extraction, the consequence was 1,300 deaths, which prompted a nationwide revision of mining safety regulations.

